  <h2>What a work order is for</h2>
  <p>
    A construction work order is the one-page document that authorizes a specific job and then
    records what it took. It is the right form for a service call, a warranty callback, a small job
    that does not need a full proposal and contract, and for directed extra work on a larger project
    where the GC says &ldquo;just handle it&rdquo; and you need something signed before you do. It
    answers the questions an invoice will be judged on later: who asked for this, what exactly was
    done, by whom, how long it took, what it used, and who accepted it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because it is both an authorization and a record, the form has two halves. The top half is
    filled before the work starts: customer, site, request, priority, how it bills, and the
    description. The bottom half is filled as the work happens: labor, materials, equipment, totals,
    completion notes, and the sign-off.
  </p>

  <h2>Work order vs. change order vs. T&amp;M ticket vs. purchase order</h2>
  <p>These four get confused constantly, and the confusion is expensive:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Document</th><th>Who issues it</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td>Work order</td><td>You</td><td
          >Authorizes a specific job and records the labor, materials and equipment it used, with
          the customer&rsquo;s acceptance.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>T&amp;M ticket</td><td>You</td><td
          >Same-day, signed record of hours, materials and equipment on directed extra work. Cost
          backup only. <a href="/templates/tm-ticket/">T&amp;M ticket template</a>.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>Change order</td><td>Both parties</td><td
          >Amends the contract sum and time. The only one of the four that changes what a contract
          pays. <a href="/templates/change-order/">Change order template</a>.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>Purchase order</td><td>The customer</td><td
          >Their commitment to buy, with their PO number. Their accounts payable department matches
          your invoice to it.</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    On a contract project, the sequence is: the GC directs the work, you open a work order or
    T&amp;M ticket and get it signed the same day, and then you price it into a change order that
    both parties sign. The work order is the backup; the change order is what gets billed on the
    next <a href="/templates/aia-g702-g703/">pay application</a>. For a standalone service job, the
    work order goes straight to a <a href="/templates/construction-invoice/">contractor invoice</a>,
    and the customer&rsquo;s PO number on the reference line is what gets it paid.
  </p>

  <h2>Field-by-field notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Requested by and reference.</strong> Name the person and cite the field directive, email,
      PO or phone call. &ldquo;Per Marcus, verbal&rdquo; is weak; &ldquo;FD-07, email 9/8&rdquo; is a
      record.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bill as.</strong> Decide before the work starts. Contract extra means a change order follows.
      T&amp;M means agreed rates. Service call means its own invoice. Warranty means no charge &mdash;
      but fill in the labor and materials anyway so you know what the callback cost.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Labor.</strong> One line per person: classification, hours and rate. Rates come from your
      contract or rate sheet, never from memory on the day.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Materials and equipment.</strong> Quantities and unit costs for parts; days or months and
      rates for rentals. If a rental runs on after the work order closes, note the start date and what
      triggers removal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Totals.</strong> Overhead and profit applies to the subtotal; sales tax applies to materials
      where your state taxes them. The Excel version does both from a rate you enter.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Completion notes.</strong> What was done, how it was tested, and anything left open. This
      is the paragraph a customer reads before signing, and the one your office reads before invoicing.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    The preview shows the completed example that ships with the download. A mechanical subcontractor
    gets an urgent request from the GC&rsquo;s project manager, backed by a field directive: provide
    temporary cooling to a server room so the owner can install network equipment before the
    permanent rooftop unit starts up. Work order WO-0187 records the request on September 9, 2026,
    with a due date two days out and the job assigned to a sheet metal journeyman and an apprentice.
    On September 10 they set a 5-ton portable spot cooler, duct the exhaust to the plenum, and pump
    the condensate to a floor sink: 12 labor hours ($900.00), $273.00 of materials, and a $795.00
    monthly rental. With 15% overhead and profit and 8.81% sales tax on the materials, the work
    order totals $2,287.25. The completion notes say the unit tested at 68&deg;F with the
    owner&rsquo;s IT lead present, and that the work bills as CO-004 on the next pay application.
    Both signatures are dated the day the work was done.
  </p>

  <h2>Using the Excel workbook</h2>
  <p>
    The Work Order sheet mirrors the form with amber input cells: hours × rate, quantity × unit cost
    and quantity × rate compute each line; the section totals, subtotal, overhead &amp; profit at
    the rate you enter, sales tax on materials, and the work order total all calculate. Priority,
    bill-as and status are dropdowns. The Work Order Log sheet takes one row per work order and
    gives you the counts that matter: open work orders, work complete but not yet billed, the
    unbilled value, and the billed-to-date total. A due date turns red when it has passed and the
    work order has no completed date. Every formula has been checked with real numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes this form is built to prevent</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Doing the work on a phone call with nothing written down.</li>
    <li>Leaving bill-as blank, then arguing later about whether it was warranty or an extra.</li>
    <li>Pricing from memory instead of the rate sheet.</li>
    <li>Getting the sign-off a week later from someone who did not see the work.</li>
    <li>
      Treating a signed work order as a change order on a contract job — on contract work, extra
      scope goes through <a href="/blog/construction-change-order-management/"
        >change order management</a
      >.
    </li>
    <li>Letting complete-not-billed work orders pile up in a folder.</li>
  </ul>
